<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead"><strong>Drop onto both feet just before your opponent hits the shuttle, and land as or just after they do.</strong> That is the split step, and it is the single habit that changes how much court a club player covers. Everything else is a map: six corners you move to, four ways of travelling between them, and one lunge that needs your knee tracking over your foot. Start moving when the shuttle leaves their racket and you are already about a quarter of a second behind, whatever your fitness.</p>

      <p>You see the smash coming, you have time to think about it, and your feet are still flat on the floor when it goes past you.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:30px">
      <h4>The short version</h4>
      <ul style="margin:10px 0 0">
        <li><strong>Take off before they hit it, land as or after they do.</strong> The split step straddles their contact. If you are still rising as the shuttle leaves, you are late.</li>
        <li><strong>It is a shallow drop, not a jump.</strong> Badminton Insight puts the ceiling at about the width of a racket grip.</li>
        <li><strong>The foot that lands first decides where you go.</strong> Left first to move right, right first to move left, front first to move back.</li>
        <li><strong>There is no all-purpose split step.</strong> BWF says so directly: you land in a way that covers the replies you think are coming.</li>
        <li><strong>Chassé is for short distances only.</strong> The syllabus says using it across the diagonals is slow and should be discouraged.</li>
        <li><strong>The lunge is where the injuries are.</strong> Lead foot, lead knee and the direction you are hitting all face the same way.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:22px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem">Written for UK club-level players who have never had formal coaching. Technique here is attributed to the BWF coaching syllabus and to named coaches; we do not teach it in our own voice. Where published evidence is thinner than the confidence around it, we say so.</p>

    <div class="guide-toc" style="margin-top:26px">
