      <h2 id="related">Where to go next</h2>
      <p>This guide covers how one player moves. Where two players stand as a pair, and when that changes, is a different subject: <a href="/guides/badminton-doubles-positioning">badminton doubles positioning</a> covers the two shapes, how to switch between them, and who takes the shuttle down the middle.</p>
      <p>If you are wondering whether your racket is holding your movement back, it almost certainly is not, but <a href="/guides/how-to-choose-a-badminton-racket">how to choose a badminton racket</a> explains why head-heavy will not speed up your smash either.</p>
      <p>And if the games themselves are the problem rather than your feet, <a href="/guides/mixed-ability-badminton-club-night">running a mixed-ability club night</a> deals with the pairing side of it.</p>

      <div style="margin-top:34px;padding-top:22px;border-top:1px solid var(--line)">
        <h4 style="margin:0 0 8px">How this guide was put together</h4>
        <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem;margin:0">ePegboard builds club-night software. We are not coaches, we have not tested any of this, and nothing here is taught in our own voice. It is compiled from the BWF Coaches' Manual Level 1, from published coaching material by Greg and Jenny Mairs of Badminton Insight, both former England internationals and English National Champions, and from published sports-science work on the split step and the badminton lunge.</p>
        <p style="color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem;margin:12px 0 0">Where the sources disagree we have said so rather than picking a side, and where the evidence is thinner than the confidence around it we have said that too: the split step's timing rule is coaching doctrine rather than a measured result, the usual physiological explanation for it is an inference, and the precise timing figures in circulation come from tennis. How long any of it takes to learn is not published anywhere we could find.</p>
